lesson k-rhymes v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=7 subject=reading levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Words that rhyme"
  goal "Hear when two words rhyme, pick a word that rhymes, and tell a rhyme from two words that only start the same."

  look {
    scene 24x14 {
      picturecards card who=["cat"] where=["on"] thing=["mat"] letters=false at=canvas(1, 1)
      text note "cat, mat" below=card gap=1
    }
    say """
      The cat is on the mat. Cat and mat rhyme: they sound the same at the end, at. Only the first
      sound changes. Cat and cup start the same, but they do not rhyme.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice k.rhyme-yes-no count=2 seed=2
    level easy {
      worked k.rhyme-pick k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice k.rhyme-pick count=2 seed=3
    practice k.rhyme-odd count=2 seed=4
    practice k.rhyme-finish count=2 seed=5 easy-count=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.rhyme-or-start k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.rhyme-number k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Rhymes sound the same at the end."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Everything here is heard, so say each word clearly and let the child say it back. The trap is a
    pair that starts the same, like cat and cup: if the child says they rhyme, say both slowly and
    stress the end. Nursery rhymes are the best practice there is, so leave a word out when you say
    one and let the child fill it in. Japan, 幼稚園教育要領 (kindergarten guidelines), 言葉, 内容 (7):
    "生活の中で言葉の楽しさや美しさに気付く。" (Notice the fun and beauty of words in daily life.)
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the pairs to judge all rhyme, the rhyme for cat is chosen in the teacher's pen first, and the words are short and common."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the pairs to judge do not rhyme, the words are longer, and the three-star's number is six or nine."
  }
}
